Probe contracts and interpretation
----------------------------------
All pointers are opaque identities, valid only during the call. Profiles never
dereference saved objects. User strings are copied only while their site fires.
No guest RAM, Bluetooth packets, cryptographic keys, fingerprints, capability
message payloads or mesh decrypted payloads are emitted.

bhyve checkpoint-phase-start(operation, phase), checkpoint-phase-done(operation,
phase, result, saved_errno): nesting is synchronous on a thread. operation is
__func__; phase is the called routine or device name. Result is the underlying
return value. saved_errno is meaningful for negative-return phases; positive
errno APIs carry the error directly in result. vm_snapshot_mem normalizes zero
bytes to -1 and nonzero to 0. Both save and restore can contain nested phases.
checkpoint-begin(stop), checkpoint-result(stop, published, error) cover the
request, including failures before file creation. A successful stop emits its
result before vm_destroy/exit. checkpoint-publish(published, error) distinguishes
a visible manifest rename from a subsequent durability failure. restore-begin
(context), restore-result(error) cover vm_restore_transaction, including invalid
arguments and missing startup fence. These do not certify guest resume if a
later bhyverun operation fails.

migration-progress(phase, round, bytes, dirty_pages, converged) reports the
existing progress milestones, not a promise of one event per packet. The source
and destination use the existing enum values. migration-send(type, sequence,
payload_length, error) fires after frame encoding/send was attempted; sequence
is the last successfully sent sequence (not the failed frame's sequence on
error). Allocation/oversize rejection occurs before this site. Existing protocol
validation tests cover failed commit, cancellation, truncated device state and
source rollback. Device-part events report accepted records only; they do not
assert a whole restore subsequently commits. Admin quiesce start/done surround
the entire active-command drain under the owner's mutex, including a zero wait.

blued discovery-lease-start(fd, generation, request), discovery-lease-done(fd,
generation, request, status, reply) use the generation to avoid fd reuse aliasing.
Discovery deliver(fd, request, adapter_index, rssi) is the delivery attempt after
filter/dedup. iso-stream-alloc/unlink use the registry object identity; unlink
is not necessarily final memory destruction. iso-handoff(stream, handle, fd)
reports attempted socket acquisition, with fd < 0 denoting failure. Existing
iso-*-status contracts report controller status after a complete HCI reply;
transport failures are visible through the existing HCI command probes. Reject,
terminate and data-path setup contracts fire on command success. BIG completion
and sync events precede stream matching; unmatched events remain observable.
Connection state reports old/new state after the state store.

btaudio command-start(fd, command, transaction), command-done(fd, command,
transaction, result): negative errno convention, with the 4-bit transaction label
interpreted only within the socket/operation. Covers synchronous AVDTP command
transactions, including timeout, rejection, malformed acceptance and peer loss;
it is not per-audio-sample tracing. HCI kernel timeout(unit, handle, state,
link_type) fires after a pending timer and live connection have been verified.

Mesh manager-transaction(node_addr, expected_opcode, state, attempts) fires after
a sent initial request, matching completion, successful retry or budget timeout.
bridge-forward(src,dst,rx_net_idx,tx_net_idx) reports selected forwarding only.
directed-expire(origin,target) precedes clearing the expired entry;
directed-control(opcode,length) follows basic pointer validation, before semantic
dispatch. remote-timeout(link) is an opening deadline; remote-transfer(link,
number,length) is a constructed outbound provisioning PDU. lpn-state(address,
state) includes initialized IDLE and transition outcomes. Existing mesh-prefixed
keyrefresh, friend, proxy, cfg and model contracts retain their original names.
The AppKey event carries indices and opcode only, not the key bytes.

meshd bearer-queue(fd,type,payload_length,pending_bytes) fires after enqueue,
before an immediate flush; bearer-full(fd,frame_length,pending_bytes) is ENOBUFS;
bearer-failed(fd,state) precedes transport cleanup. persist-write-start(length),
persist-write-done(length,result) cover writing an already-created temporary file
through durable publication/cleanup; validation/allocation/temp-file failures
occur earlier. Result is 0/-1, not errno (cleanup may change errno).

ntsync wait-start(state,count,all), wait-done(state,wait_error,object_error,index)
cover the common wait implementation used by native and Linux ioctl callers.
Index is -1 if the wait fails; object_error separately includes abandoned-owner
semantics. create(object,fd,error) covers descriptor installation (fd=-1 on error),
not earlier allocation failures. inotify wait-start(instance), wait-done(instance,
error) measure one sleep iteration; spurious wakes can produce several pairs.
watch-add(instance,wd,mask,error) includes updates, but not failures before a
validated inotify file is acquired. Earlier queue/remove probes remain intact.

Coalition cleanup-status(coalition,phase,error) is a status computation, not
necessarily a transition. waiter-create/revoke(coalition,instance) bracket waiter
membership; waiter-notify(coalition,instance,phase) means the terminal notification
was successfully queued, not that a client consumed it. Pending-count fields
remain available through the existing status API rather than leaking objects.

MACF syscall-start(policy,call), syscall-done(policy,call,error) cover dispatch,
including no matching policy; copyinstr and capability-mode rejection occur
before dispatch. Existing per-policy hook and decision probes already cover
active policy callbacks. mac_do configuration(jail,rules_changed,paths_changed,
error) reports parse/set outcome. rule-grant(old_uid,new_uid,flags,error) reports
each applicable rule's decision, not a combined grant from every policy.
veriexec fingerprint-status(vnode,status) reports the prior cached status;
fingerprint-start(vnode,size), fingerprint-done(vnode,size,error) surround hashing
only when metadata requests it. Status and failure enums remain those of veriexec.

Safety, active code and intentional boundaries
---------------------------------------------
Kernel probes use SDT's disabled asm-goto NOP sites and compile away without
KDTRACE_HOOKS. No allocation, string formatting or new locks are added by probes.
The VMM sites are at the process-context snapshot dispatcher, after selector
validation and outside VM-entry, VM-exit, FPU/TSC restore internals, NMI/IPI,
interrupt-entry, bootstrap, pmap, allocator and DTrace implementation paths.
The new HCI timer site runs through Netgraph's established callback context.
These probes cannot observe execution before DTrace providers are registered.
IRQ thread and VM-object probes from COVERAGE-FIFTH retain their original limits.

Descriptor audit also checked existing envfd create/read/write/seal/close,
jaildesc alloc/find/get/set/close, process descriptors, capability channels and
Linux pidfd/signalfd/perf coverage. No duplicate lifecycle probes were added just
to increase counts. DTYPE_NTSYNC and coalition cleanup descriptors had real gaps.
Active MAC callbacks are covered through MACF; ABAC, isolation, authority and
other capability providers retain their earlier semantic events. The retained
mac_grantbylabel source has no current module Makefile and refers to an obsolete
mpo_proc_check_resource hook. It is not claimed as built coverage and was left
unchanged. Likewise removed policy directories are not counted as installed code.

Matter/OpenThread boundary: a7b358373a7 imported Matter Python IDL tooling/data
and OpenThread build targets. contrib/connectedhomeip/README.5BSD and
contrib/openthread/README.5BSD explicitly state these are not installed capability
services. There is no native Matter commissioning/device runtime here to place
service probes in. No fictitious Matter or physical Thread provider is added.
All 120 imported Matter IDL tests were run successfully. They verify tooling,
not commissioning, radio operation or Matter interoperability. OpenThread's
simulation/tool builds are not represented as installed base services.

